Q: Explanation of the Java logic behind this StringBuilder code A: <p>In Java there are <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/tutorial/java/nutsandbolts/datatypes.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">primitive types</a> and classes. Primitive types, such as <code>int</code>, <code>char</code>, etc. have values and you can do some operations with them, but they are not instances of a class, just some values you can work with. Consequently, they have no <a href="https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/java/methods-in-java/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">methods</a> either. A method is a function attached to a <code>class</code> or an instance of a <code>class</code> (depending on whether it is <code>static</code> or not). A <code>class</code> is a general behavior that defines a pattern of state both statically and on instance-level, <code>static</code> members and methods belonging to the class, while non-<code>static</code> members and methods belonging to an instance. For example, if you have a class <code>Foo</code> and you instantiated it as <code>foo</code>, then <code>Foo</code> is the class and <code>foo</code> is the object:</p>
<pre><code>Foo foo = new foo();
</code></pre>
<p>In the code above we <strong>instantiate</strong> <code>Foo</code>, that is, we create a new <code>Foo</code> <strong>object</strong> and we call it <code>foo</code>. Now, if you have a static <code>f</code> method and an instance-level <code>g</code> method, then you can call <code>g</code> via <code>foo.g()</code>, but in order to call <code>f</code> you'll need to refer to the class and call it via <code>Foo.f()</code>. With this being said, let's see what confused you:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When I first attempted it there was and issue in first and second if statement saying I can't use charAt() in arraylist when I used arraylist I couldn't use String.join()</p>
</blockquote>
<ul>
<li><code>ArrayList</code> is a class and does not have a method called <code>charAt</code></li>
<li><code>String</code> is another class and has a <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/lang/String.html#charAt-int-" rel="nofollow noreferrer">charAt</a> method</li>
<li>So your first problem was that you had an <code>ArrayList&lt;String&gt;</code> and you tried to call <code>charAt</code> on the <code>ArrayList</code>, which has no such method instead on its elements, which, being strings have such a method</li>
<li><a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/lang/Character.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Character</a> is a wrapper class for the primitive <code>char</code> type and has no method called <code>charAt</code></li>
<li>At this point I'm unsure how you called <code>String.join</code>, but we have a definition <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/lang/String.html#join-java.lang.CharSequence-java.lang.CharSequence...-" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a> and one <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/lang/String.html#join-java.lang.CharSequence-java.lang.Iterable-" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a> for <code>String.join</code>, so you either need to pass two <code>CharSequence</code> objects to it, or a <code>CharSequence</code> object and an <code>Iterable</code> of <code>CharSequence</code>-extending class instance, since <code>ArrayList&lt;Character&gt;</code> nor <code>Character</code> is a <code>CharSequence</code>, because it is a different class, passing <code>ArrayList&lt;Character&gt;</code> or <code>Character</code> to <code>String.join</code> will fail</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>I don't understand the logic behind it can someone tell me where I can look for it in detail. Comments would help a lot too.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You refer to this line</p>
<pre><code>mergedWord.add(word1.charAt(i++) + &quot;&quot;); // this plus and double quotes.
</code></pre>
<p>(and its twin with <code>word2</code>, but the explanation is the same)</p>
<p>So here, you have <code>mergeWord</code>, an <code>ArrayList&lt;String&gt;</code>, whose <code>.add()</code> method is being invoked, adding an element to the <code>ArrayList</code>. The element you add is the <code>i</code>'th character of <code>word1</code>, a <code>String</code>, whose <code>charAt</code> method is being invoked, passing <code>i++</code> to it, which yields a <code>char</code>. In order to be able to add the <code>char</code> you retrieved, you convert the result to a <code>String</code> via the <code>+ &quot;&quot;</code> part so <code>.add()</code> will get a <code>String</code> parameter. <code>i++</code> yields <code>i</code>'s current value and increments the variable afterwards, so it will be greater by exactly 1 when you next refer to <code>i</code>.</p>
<p>You can call <code>.add()</code> for the <code>ArrayList</code> instance without any issues, because such an instance has such a method. Similarly, you can invoke <code>charAt</code> on the <code>String</code> instance, because such an instance has such a method. So, let's see the bigger picture:</p>
<pre><code>        while(i &lt; word1.length() || j &lt; word2.length()){
            if(i &lt; word1.length()){
                mergedWord.add(word1.charAt(i++) + &quot;&quot;); // this here
            }
            if(j &lt; word2.length()){
                mergedWord.add(word2.charAt(j++) + &quot;&quot;); // and here
            }
        }
</code></pre>
<p>Here you iterate with <code>i</code> and <code>j</code> until either one hits the limit of <code>word1</code> or <code>word2</code>, respectively and <code>mergedWord</code>, an <code>ArrayList</code> gets a new <code>String</code> element. So you join your <code>String</code>s of the <code>ArrayList</code> together at the end. However, this is not an ideal solution. You don't need an <code>ArrayList</code> for this problem, you don't need to convert each character to a <code>String</code> and append to the <code>ArrayList</code> just to join it to a <code>String</code> at the end. Instead you could use a <code>String</code> and concatenate:</p>
<pre><code>class Solution {
    public String mergeAlternately(String word1, String word2) {
        String mergedWord = &quot;&quot;;
        int i = 0;
        int j = 0;

        while(i &lt; word1.length() || j &lt; word2.length()){
            if(i &lt; word1.length()){
                mergedWord += word1.charAt(i++); // this here
            }
            if(j &lt; word2.length()){
                mergedWord += word2.charAt(j++); // this here
            }
        }
        return mergedWord;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note that you are not using <code>StringBuilder</code> in your solution, apart from an unused comment, but you use an <code>ArrayList</code>.</p>

Q: Should (double)(std::float16_t(1024) + std::float16_t(1025)) be 2048.0 or 2049.0? A: <p>Intermediate higher precision can already bite you with plan <code>double</code>s:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;print&gt;

int main() {
    volatile double k = 2877.0;
    double v = k / 1000000.0;
    std::print(&quot;{}\n&quot;, v);
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>On godbolt:</p>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/bWqxYadGG" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://godbolt.org/z/bWqxYadGG</a></p>
<p>In the upper compiler this is compiled on Linux x86_64 with SSE2 instructions (which use exact double rounding rules, even for intermediates), while the lower compiler is the same, but compiled as a 32bit executable, and using the 387-compatible FPU instructions (which uses 80bit &quot;extended double&quot; for intermediate results). When storing the second one in a 64 bit double value it is rounded again (from the intermediate value), and results in a different result by one bit as compared to the calculation done with only doubles. (Basically this is the result of two seqeuntial rounding operations - first the &quot;infinite&quot; result is rounded by the hardware to 80bit extended precision, and then that value is rounded to double - compared to the &quot;infinite&quot; result being rounded to double directly.)</p>
<p>You can even switch the FPU mode for the 387 FPU to have it work in double precision only to get the same result as with modern SSE instructions:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#if defined(USE_FPU_CONTROL)
#include &lt;fpu_control.h&gt;

//  ... 
    fpu_control_t fpu_oldcw, fpu_cw;
    _FPU_GETCW(fpu_oldcw); // store old cw
    fpu_cw = (fpu_oldcw &amp; ~_FPU_EXTENDED &amp; ~_FPU_DOUBLE &amp; ~_FPU_SINGLE) | _FPU_DOUBLE;
    _FPU_SETCW(fpu_cw);
#endif
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/fdT1qh3eW" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://godbolt.org/z/fdT1qh3eW</a></p>
<p>(Windows btw. uses the FPU mode with double precision as the default, while Linux defaults to extended precision.)</p>
<p>Of course people compiling for 32bit x86 without SSE is exceedingly rare (even 32bit games will typically require SSE nowadays), so this specific issue with <code>double</code> is unlikely to pop up in practice.</p>
<p>But with the newer floating point types, especially if they aren't supported fully in hardware and must be emulated using larger types, these kinds of issues can easily reoccur.</p>
